// csharp-rt.metajs -- C#'S OWN SEMANTICS, in MetaJS. Layer 2 of
// docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3, for the fifth language after the Lua pilot,
// PHP, Swift and Java.
//
// languages/csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits handle IR that calls 68 js_* externs for
// tests/csharp-test-full.cs. 35 of them are generic primitives
// languages/lib/runtime.c already provides (the C floor, layer 1). The other 33
// are here.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/csharp-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-csharp-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/csharp-rt.ll that csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf links next to runtime.ll.
// -rt-lib exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` below under its own C symbol, so
// nothing else in this file is visible to the linker - the helpers are
// deliberately named cs*/cp*.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrtcsharp.go, plus abnf/jsrtjvm.go for
// the boxed double and the js_csadd / js_cslen / js_char / js_char_code /
// js_jchareq / js_jadd / js_mcall / js_supercall / js_is_type / js_arr_new_n /
// js_dict_new / js_pyget / js_pyset / js_pylen / js_has / js_goslice /
// js_gospread block of abnf/jsrt.go. This file matches them function for
// function; where it CANNOT, the site says so in full.
//
// ----- the value model (abnf/jsrtcsharp.go's own header) ---------------------
//
//     null / undefined     ==  a null reference
//     a PLAIN NUMBER       ==  an `int`, i.e. a SIGNED 32 BIT value. This is C#'s
//                              invariant and it is deliberately NOT Go's, where a
//                              plain number is a 64 bit int.
//     a tag 13 box         ==  EVERY other integral type: long, ulong, uint,
//                              short, ushort, byte (UNSIGNED in C#) and sbyte.
//     a tag 14 box         ==  double / float / decimal, STYLE 2 (C#).
//     a {__char} box       ==  char. The Go twin uses jsChar, a Go TYPE. Layer 2
//                              cannot make a primitive type, so this is the same
//                              OBJECT box languages/csharp-interpreter.abnf and
//                              languages/lib/java-rt.metajs already use.
//     a {__dict} object    ==  Dictionary<K,V>: keys/vals parallel arrays.
//     an array             ==  int[] / List<T> alike (this runtime has one).
//     an {__isclass} obj   ==  a type descriptor: __name, __super, __isrecord.
//     any other object     ==  an instance; its descriptor is __class.
//
// ----- THE LONG PROBLEM, and how `sintRaw` removed it ------------------------
//
// C#'s `long` must stay DISTINGUISHABLE from `int` at every value, because
// `1000000L * 1000000L` is 1000000000000 while `1000000 * 1000000` wraps to
// -727379968. The floor's si_norm - the ONE place the sized-integer invariant is
// applied - UNBOXES a signed 64 bit value a double holds exactly, so
// `sint(0, 5, 64, 0)` is the plain number 5, indistinguishable from `int` 5.
//
// languages/lib/java-rt.metajs met that wall first and worked around it by
// marking a long box UNSIGNED - the one shape si_norm will not unbox - and then
// re-supplying six SIGNED operations (/ % >>, compare, decimal text, double
// reading) in about 90 lines. C# does NOT do that, because the floor now has
//
//     sintRaw(hi, lo, bits, unsigned)   si_trunc, then si_make: a tag 13 cell
//                                       ALWAYS, never si_norm's unboxing arm
//
// so a C# long is an honest {w: 64, u: 0} box. Every one of the six operations is
// then the floor's own: sintOp's `/` and `%` and `>>` already branch on the cell's
// signedness, sintCmp already compares signed when the type is, sintStr already
// prints the minus sign and sintNum already reads the negative half. csharp-rt has
// no jl* layer at all. THAT IS THE MEASUREMENT the floor request asked for:
// ~90 lines saved, and - the part that matters more - the JAVA SECOND-ORDER HAZARD
// IS GONE WITH IT. A floor extern that reads a tag 13 cell numerically (js_csflo
// is C#'s one) sees a MAGNITUDE when the cell is the marked-unsigned shape; with
// sintRaw the cell is signed, so to_number reads it correctly and no js_*num
// wrapper is owed. go / kotlin / dart should all take this route.
//
// ----- generators: the floor drives them, and layer 2 owes NOTHING -----------
//
// C# is the first language to reach the coroutine primitive that landed in the
// floor (tags 15/16). It costs this file zero lines, and the reason is worth
// copying for js / ts / python, which follow: csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf lowers the
// WHOLE iterator protocol in the EMITTER, over the floor's own member syntax -
//
//     js_genfn(js_closure(...))                 the iterator method
//     js_typeof(js_get(g, "next")) === "function"   "is this a generator?"
//     js_call(js_get(g, "next"), [])            MoveNext()
//     js_get(step, "value") / js_get(step, "done")
//
// The floor's get_member answers `next` on a tag 15 cell with a bound method, so
// every one of those is already a floor extern. `yield break` is an ordinary
// return and `.Current` is a field the emitter parks on its own cursor object.
// A THROW out of a generator body needs no case either: gen_resume re-raises it on
// the RESUMER's stack, so it comes out of the `next()` call site like any other.
//
// ----- what C# did NOT need, checked rather than assumed ---------------------
//
//  1. NO `__raw` truthy shim. `grep -n truthyExt languages/csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf`
//     is empty, so core.truthyExt keeps compile-core's default "js_truthy", which
//     is the C FLOOR'S OWN function and already answers the raw 1/0 the emitted
//     `icmp ne ..., 0` compares. Swift and Java were the same; Lua and PHP are the
//     two that override it.
//  2. ONE `_raw` PARAMETER, js_char's, exactly like Java. csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf
//     writes the code point with handle(k), and 0..3 are the handles of
//     undefined/null/false/true - so without the suffix the callee would see one of
//     those. Every other handle(k) in that grammar goes to js_closure or js_arg,
//     which are floor externs.
//  3. NO scope-probe extern to lower. C#'s emitter already probes the scope
//     in IR (js_scope_typeof against "undefined" at csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf:4109);
//     PHP's and Swift's lowering is already done here, so there was nothing left.
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local (or a reassigned parameter) keeps the TYPE
// CLASS it is first assigned, so a variable that holds a number in one branch and
// an object in another is declared `var x = anytype`; there is no ASI, so every
// `return` carries its value on the same line and a value-less early return is
// BRACED; no for..in, no splice, no split/join/lastIndexOf, indexOf ignores its
// from-index, array.length is not assignable, and there are no exponent literals.

// ===================================================== the char box =========

function csChar(code) {
    var o = {}
    o["__char"] = code
    return o
}

function csIsChar(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    return v["__char"] !== undefined
}

// ===================================================== value readings =======

// A PLAIN number is an `int`. A sized integer (tag 13) and a boxed double (tag 14)
// are both `typeof v == "number"` in the C floor, so every site that meant "an
// ordinary double" has to say so.
function csIsPlainNum(v) {
    if (typeof v != "number") { return false }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return false }
    return !floIs(v)
}

// csIsIntegral (jsrtcsharp.go): an operand of INTEGER arithmetic. A char is one,
// C#'s char being an integral type; a double is not.
function csIsIntegral(v) {
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return true }
    if (typeof v != "number") { return false }
    return !floIs(v)
}

// csRaw hands the sint* builtins something they can read. si_val knows tag 3, tag
// 13 and tag 14 - it does NOT know the {__char} object box, which would read as
// NaN, so the code point is taken off it here and nowhere else.
function csRaw(v) {
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    return v
}

// csSzW / csSzU: the declared width and signedness of an operand. A plain number
// and a char both answer "signed 32", which is what jsrtcsharp.go's csSzW/csSzU do
// by falling through their jsGInt case.
function csSzW(v) {
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintWidth(v) }
    return 32
}

function csSzU(v) {
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintUns(v) }
    return false
}

// csNorm applies the C# invariant: a signed 32 bit result is a PLAIN NUMBER,
// everything else is a box. sintConv would unbox a signed 64 bit value that a
// double holds exactly (si_norm), so the result is re-boxed with sintRaw whenever
// the invariant says it must stay one - which is the whole of what java-rt.metajs
// needed ninety lines to avoid.
function csNorm(v, w, u) {
    var t = sintConv(csRaw(v), w, u)
    if (w == 32) { if (!u) { return sintNum(t) } }
    if (sintIs(t)) { return t }
    return sintRaw(sintHi(t), sintLo(t), w, u)
}

// csBox is jsrtcsharp.go's csBox: read a value at a given width and signedness,
// ALWAYS as a box (never a plain number, whatever the invariant would say).
function csBox(v, w, u) {
    var t = sintConv(csRaw(v), w, u)
    if (sintIs(t)) { return t }
    return sintRaw(sintHi(t), sintLo(t), w, u)
}

// The double reading of anything numeric.
function csNum(v) {
    if (floIs(v)) { return floNum(v) }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintNum(v) }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return v }
    if (v === true) { return 1 }
    if (v === false) { return 0 }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return parseFloat(v) }
    return 0
}

// The int32 reading of an integral operand.
function csI(v) {
    var n = csNorm(v, 32, false)
    return n
}

// ===================================================== BCL exceptions =======

// csThrow (jsrtcsharp.go): the BCL exception types are plain objects carrying
// __excname and Message in BOTH halves - the shape `new DivideByZeroException(msg)`
// builds (csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf:3138) and a catch clause reads.
function csThrow(name, msg) {
    var o = {}
    o["__excname"] = name
    o["Message"] = msg
    throw o
}

// ===================================================== the operators ========

// csPromote is ECMA-334 12.4.7.3 binary numeric promotion for two integral
// operands. The surprising rule is the uint one: `uint + int` is a LONG, because
// int does not fit in uint and uint does not fit in int. It is the only place C#
// differs from Java's much simpler "long if either is long, else int".
function csPromote(l, r) {
    var lw = csSzW(l)
    var lu = csSzU(l)
    var rw = csSzW(r)
    var ru = csSzU(r)
    if (lw == 64 && lu) { return {w: 64, u: true} }
    if (rw == 64 && ru) { return {w: 64, u: true} }
    if (lw == 64 || rw == 64) { return {w: 64, u: false} }
    if ((lw == 32 && lu) || (rw == 32 && ru)) {
        var other = l
        if (lw == 32 && lu) { other = r }
        // A char, a byte and a ushort convert to uint and stay there; a plain
        // number is an `int` and forces the pair up to long.
        if (csIsChar(other)) { return {w: 32, u: true} }
        if (sintIs(other)) {
            if (sintUns(other)) { return {w: 32, u: true} }
            if (sintWidth(other) > 32) { return {w: 32, u: true} }
            return {w: 64, u: false}
        }
        return {w: 64, u: false}
    }
    return {w: 32, u: false}
}

// The sintOp index of one arithmetic or bitwise operator.
function csOpCode(op) {
    if (op == "+") { return 0 }
    if (op == "-") { return 1 }
    if (op == "*") { return 2 }
    if (op == "/") { return 3 }
    if (op == "%") { return 4 }
    if (op == "&") { return 5 }
    if (op == "|") { return 6 }
    if (op == "^") { return 7 }
    return 0 - 1
}

// csArith: one binary arithmetic or bitwise operator on two INTEGRAL operands, at
// the promoted type and wrapping to it. ARITHMETIC IS UNCHECKED, C#'s default
// (ECMA-334 12.8.20): every operator wraps. Division by zero DOES throw (12.9.3).
function csArith(op, l, r) {
    var p = csPromote(l, r)
    var w = p.w
    var un = p.u
    var code = csOpCode(op)
    if (code < 0) {
        fail("js_csarith: unknown operator '" + op + "'")
        return undefined
    }
    if (w != 32 || un) {
        var a = csBox(l, w, un)
        var b = csBox(r, w, un)
        if (op == "/" || op == "%") {
            if (sintCmp(b, csBox(0, w, un)) == 0) {
                csThrow("DivideByZeroException", "Attempted to divide by zero.")
            }
        }
        return csNorm(sintOp(code, a, b), w, un)
    }
    var x = csRaw(l)
    var y = csRaw(r)
    var xi = csNorm(x, 32, false)
    var yi = csNorm(y, 32, false)
    if (op == "+") { return csWrap32(xi + yi) }
    if (op == "-") { return csWrap32(xi - yi) }
    if (op == "*") { return Math.imul(xi, yi) }
    if (op == "/") {
        if (yi == 0) { csThrow("DivideByZeroException", "Attempted to divide by zero.") }
        // int.MinValue / -1 overflows. ECMA-334 12.9.3 leaves the UNCHECKED outcome
        // implementation-defined between a System.OverflowException and "the
        // resulting value being that of the left operand"; the second is what BOTH
        // halves answer, and it is what Java specifies outright.
        if (xi == 0 - 2147483648) { if (yi == 0 - 1) { return xi } }
        return csWrap32(Math.trunc(xi / yi))
    }
    if (op == "%") {
        if (yi == 0) { csThrow("DivideByZeroException", "Attempted to divide by zero.") }
        if (xi == 0 - 2147483648) { if (yi == 0 - 1) { return 0 } }
        return xi - Math.trunc(xi / yi) * yi
    }
    if (op == "&") { return xi & yi }
    if (op == "|") { return xi | yi }
    return xi ^ yi
}

// The 32 bit signed wrap of a double, which is what an int-width result reduces to.
function csWrap32(x) {
    if (x !== x) { return 0 }
    if (x == Infinity) { return 0 }
    if (x == 0 - Infinity) { return 0 }
    var m = x - Math.floor(x / 4294967296) * 4294967296
    if (m >= 2147483648) { m = m - 4294967296 }
    return m
}

// csShift is << >> >>>. The result type is the LEFT operand's alone after unary
// promotion (ECMA-334 12.11: sbyte/byte/short/ushort/char all become int), and the
// COUNT is MASKED - & 31 for an int/uint, & 63 for a long/ulong, so `1 << 32` is 1.
// `>>` on an UNSIGNED type is the LOGICAL shift, which is how C# spells what Java
// writes as `>>>`; C# 11's own `>>>` is that same operation on a signed type.
//
// Every arm goes through sintOp on a BOX rather than through MetaJS's own 32 bit
// operators, so the signed/unsigned choice is made once, by the cell's own flag.
function csShift(op, l, r) {
    var w = 32
    if (csSzW(l) == 64) { w = 64 }
    var un = false
    if (csSzW(l) >= 32) { un = csSzU(l) }
    var s = csShiftCount(r, w)
    if (op == ">>>") {
        // The logical shift whatever the declared signedness is: read the same bits
        // through an UNSIGNED cell of the same width.
        return csNorm(sintOp(10, csBox(l, w, true), s), w, un)
    }
    var a = csBox(l, w, un)
    if (op == "<<") { return csNorm(sintOp(9, a, s), w, un) }
    return csNorm(sintOp(10, a, s), w, un)
}

// giVal(r) & 31 / & 63. The count is read at 32 bits first, which is what every
// count in a C# program is, and then masked.
function csShiftCount(r, w) {
    var n = csNorm(r, 32, false)
    if (w == 64) { return n & 63 }
    return n & 31
}

// csCmp is the ordered comparison at the PROMOTED type. sintCmp on its own is not
// usable: it compares at the widest operand's width, so `byte b = 5; b < 1000`
// would truncate 1000 to a byte and answer false.
function csCmp(op, l, r) {
    var p = csPromote(l, r)
    var c = sintCmp(csBox(l, p.w, p.u), csBox(r, p.w, p.u))
    if (op == "<") { return c < 0 }
    if (op == ">") { return c > 0 }
    if (op == "<=") { return c <= 0 }
    if (op == ">=") { return c >= 0 }
    if (op == "==") { return c == 0 }
    return c != 0
}

// csConvTo is an explicit conversion to an integral type. An INTEGRAL source is a
// pure bit truncation (unchecked); a FLOATING POINT one whose value does not fit is
// "undefined behaviour" in ECMA-334 12.3.2, so this SATURATES - deterministic, and
// identical in both halves.
function csConvTo(v, w, u) {
    if (floIs(v)) {
        var n = floNum(v)
        if (n !== n) { n = 0 }
        if (n >= 18446744073709551616) { return csNorm(csAllOnes(), w, u) }
        if (n >= 9223372036854775808) {
            if (u) { return csNorm(csFromUDouble(n), w, u) }
            return csNorm(csMaxI64(), w, u)
        }
        if (n < 0 - 9223372036854775808) { return csNorm(csMinI64(), w, u) }
        return csNorm(csFromDouble(Math.trunc(n)), w, u)
    }
    return csNorm(v, w, u)
}

// ulong.MaxValue's bit pattern, i.e. Go's `p = -1`.
function csAllOnes() { return sintRaw(4294967295, 4294967295, 64, true) }
function csMaxI64() { return sintRaw(2147483647, 4294967295, 64, false) }
function csMinI64() { return sintRaw(2147483648, 0, 64, false) }

// An integral double in [-2^63, 2^63) as a 64 bit cell. Above 2^53 the double is
// already inexact and this reproduces that inexactness rather than inventing
// digits, which is what int64(math.Trunc(n)) does in the Go twin.
function csFromDouble(v) {
    var neg = false
    var a = v
    if (a < 0) {
        neg = true
        a = 0 - a
    }
    var hi = Math.floor(a / 4294967296)
    var lo = a - hi * 4294967296
    var x = sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, false)
    if (neg) { x = sintOp(1, sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false), x) }
    return x
}

// uint64(n) for the [2^63, 2^64) half, where the signed reading would overflow.
function csFromUDouble(v) {
    var hi = Math.floor(v / 4294967296)
    var lo = v - hi * 4294967296
    return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, true)
}

// ===================================================== the boxed double =====

// jvmArith (abnf/jsrtjvm.go), reached whenever a double is on either side. floOp is
// the floor's own tag 14 arm of exactly this, by operator INDEX: 0 + 1 - 2 * 3 / 4 %.
function csFloArith(op, l, r) {
    var code = 0
    if (op == "-") { code = 1 } else if (op == "*") { code = 2 } else if (op == "/") { code = 3 } else if (op == "%") { code = 4 }
    return floOp(code, csFloOperand(l), csFloOperand(r))
}

// floOp reads its operands with the floor's to_number, which knows tag 13 and tag
// 14 but NOT the {__char} object box. A tag 13 cell is read correctly - a C# long
// is an honest signed cell, unlike java-rt.metajs's marked-unsigned one - so only
// the char needs converting here.
function csFloOperand(v) {
    if (floIs(v)) { return v }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    return v
}

// The shared jsCompare (abnf/jsrt.go:1490): two strings compare as strings,
// everything else as numbers.
//
// THE `2` IS NOT A TYPO AND IT MUST NOT BE SMOOTHED TO 0. jsCompare answers the
// SENTINEL 2 for a NaN operand, commented there as "every relation is false" -
// but js_cscmp, which is the only caller C# reaches, does not test for it and
// compares the sentinel like an ordinary ordering:
//
//     <   c < 0    false      <=  c <= 0   false
//     >   c > 0    TRUE       >=  c >= 0   TRUE
//
// so `double.NaN > 0.0` is TRUE under llvm.Run and `double.NaN <= 0.0` is false.
// Real C# (ECMA-334 12.12.1) says every one of the four is FALSE. Returning 0
// here instead - the obvious reading - makes `>` false and `<=` TRUE, which is
// wrong in a DIFFERENT way and breaks byte-identity: measured, 16 lines of the
// 19,388-line probe below. The two halves have to agree first; the shared
// sentinel bug is recorded in docs/runtime-next-plan.md and is not fixable from
// layer 2 alone.
function csCompare(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string") {
        if (typeof r == "string") {
            if (l < r) { return 0 - 1 }
            if (l > r) { return 1 }
            return 0
        }
    }
    var a = csNum(l)
    var b = csNum(r)
    if (a !== a) { return 2 }
    if (b !== b) { return 2 }
    if (a < b) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (a > b) { return 1 }
    return 0
}

// ===================================================== rendering ============

// cspBoolStr is System.Boolean.ToString(): Boolean.TrueString / Boolean.FalseString,
// which are the strings "True" and "False" (the LITERALS true / false are unchanged
// - only the string CONVERSION is capitalised).
function cpBoolStr(b) {
    if (b) { return "True" }
    return "False"
}

// cspStr: the full C# string conversion, the twin of csStr in
// csharp-interpreter.abnf and of cspRender in abnf/jsrtcsharp.go.
function cpStr(v) { return cpRender(v, 0) }

function cpRender(v, depth) {
    if (depth > 32) { return "..." }
    // Console.WriteLine(null) writes an empty line (ECMA-334 12.4.7).
    if (v === undefined) { return "" }
    if (v === null) { return "" }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return cpBoolStr(v) }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return String.fromCharCode(v["__char"]) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return floStr(v) }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintStr(v) }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return "" + v }
    if (typeof v == "function") { return "" + v }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return cpArrayName(v) }
    var s = cpObjStr(v, depth)
    if (s !== undefined) { return s }
    return cpPlainObj(v)
}

// The Go twin's fallthrough for an object shape it does not know is rt.toString,
// which is JavaScript's "[object Object]" - a Dictionary and a tuple land here, and
// both halves already agreed there.
function cpPlainObj(o) { return "[object Object]" }

// cpObjStr renders the object shapes the two C# grammars build, and answers
// undefined for one it does not know.
function cpObjStr(o, depth) {
    // A user ToString() override wins over every built-in rendering, exactly as the
    // virtual System.Object.ToString() does in C#.
    var m = cpFindToString(o)
    if (m !== undefined) { return cpRender(m.apply(undefined, [o]), depth + 1) }
    // The type descriptor itself.
    if (o["__isclass"] !== undefined) {
        var n = o["__name"]
        if (typeof n == "string") { return n }
    }
    // An instance: its type's BARE name (see the header of abnf/jsrtcsharp.go -
    // real C# prints the fully qualified name, which this runtime has no concept of).
    var cls = o["__class"]
    if (cls === undefined) { return undefined }
    if (cls === null) { return undefined }
    if (typeof cls != "object") { return undefined }
    var cn = cls["__name"]
    if (typeof cn == "string") { if (cn.length > 0) { return cn } }
    return undefined
}

// cpFindToString walks the __class / __super chain for a user-declared ToString,
// the same walk the method dispatch does in both C# grammars.
function cpFindToString(o) {
    var cls = o["__class"]
    var guard = 0
    while (guard < 64) {
        guard = guard + 1
        if (cls === undefined) { return undefined }
        if (cls === null) { return undefined }
        if (typeof cls != "object") { return undefined }
        var m = cls["ToString"]
        if (m !== undefined) { if (typeof m == "function") { return m } }
        cls = cls["__super"]
    }
    return undefined
}

// cspArrayName is Object.ToString() of an array: the element type's full name plus
// "[]". The DECLARED element type is not carried by this value model, so it is
// INFERRED from the elements - see the header of abnf/jsrtcsharp.go for the rule
// and for what real C# would answer instead.
function cpArrayName(a) {
    if (a.length == 0) { return "System.Object[]" }
    var name = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        var n = cpElemName(a[i])
        if (n == "") { return "System.Object[]" }
        if (name != "") { if (n != name) { return "System.Object[]" } }
        name = n
        i = i + 1
    }
    return name + "[]"
}

function cpElemName(e) {
    if (csIsChar(e)) { return "System.Char" }
    if (typeof e == "boolean") { return "System.Boolean" }
    if (typeof e == "string") { return "System.String" }
    if (floIs(e)) { return "System.Double" }
    if (sintIs(e)) {
        // A sized integer carries its own type, so long[] / uint[] / byte[] are the
        // one array kind whose element type is not a guess.
        var w = sintWidth(e)
        var u = sintUns(e)
        if (w == 64) { return u ? "System.UInt64" : "System.Int64" }
        if (w == 32) { return u ? "System.UInt32" : "System.Int32" }
        if (w == 16) { return u ? "System.UInt16" : "System.Int16" }
        return u ? "System.Byte" : "System.SByte"
    }
    if (typeof e == "number") { return "System.Int32" }
    return ""
}

// csString (abnf/jsrt.go): the rendering js_csadd / js_csadd2 apply to an operand of
// '+'. A null operand contributes the EMPTY STRING (ECMA-334 12.4.7), everything
// else goes through the shared toString - which for the values js_csstr has already
// let through is the number, the sized integer, the boxed double or the char.
function cpAddStr(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return "" }
    if (v === null) { return "" }
    return cpToString(v)
}

// The shared runtime's own toString, which is what rt.toString answers for each of
// the shapes that can still reach it here.
function cpToString(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return v ? "true" : "false" }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return String.fromCharCode(v["__char"]) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return floStr(v) }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintStr(v) }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return "" + v }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return cpJoin(v) }
    return "" + v
}

// rt.toString of an array is its elements joined with a comma. There is no join in
// this dialect, so it is written out.
function cpJoin(a) {
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        if (i > 0) { out = out + "," }
        out = out + cpToString(a[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// ===================================================== the integral types ===

// csTypeW / csTypeU: C#'s `byte` is UNSIGNED (0..255) and `sbyte` is the signed one
// - the opposite of Java, and the single most common way to get this wrong. The
// names are prefixed so that no property of Object.prototype can be hit by mistake.
function csTypeW(name) {
    if (name == "sbyte") { return 8 }
    if (name == "byte") { return 8 }
    if (name == "short") { return 16 }
    if (name == "ushort") { return 16 }
    if (name == "int") { return 32 }
    if (name == "uint") { return 32 }
    if (name == "long") { return 64 }
    if (name == "ulong") { return 64 }
    if (name == "nint") { return 64 }
    if (name == "nuint") { return 64 }
    return 0
}

function csTypeU(name) {
    if (name == "byte") { return true }
    if (name == "ushort") { return true }
    if (name == "uint") { return true }
    if (name == "ulong") { return true }
    if (name == "nuint") { return true }
    return false
}

function csTypeAlias(name) {
    if (name == "SByte") { return "sbyte" }
    if (name == "Byte") { return "byte" }
    if (name == "Int16") { return "short" }
    if (name == "UInt16") { return "ushort" }
    if (name == "Int32") { return "int" }
    if (name == "UInt32") { return "uint" }
    if (name == "Int64") { return "long" }
    if (name == "UInt64") { return "ulong" }
    return name
}

// csParseInt is int.Parse / long.Parse / uint.Parse: a decimal digit run with an
// optional sign, and a System.FormatException on anything else.
function csParseInt(s, w, u) {
    var t = s.trim()
    var neg = false
    var i = 0
    if (t.length > 0) {
        var c0 = t.charAt(0)
        if (c0 == "+") { i = 1 } else if (c0 == "-") {
            neg = true
            i = 1
        }
    }
    if (i >= t.length) {
        csThrow("FormatException", "The input string was not in a correct format.")
    }
    var acc = sintRaw(0, 0, 64, true)
    var ten = sintRaw(0, 10, 64, true)
    while (i < t.length) {
        var d = t.charCodeAt(i) - 48
        if (d < 0 || d > 9) {
            csThrow("FormatException", "The input string was not in a correct format.")
        }
        acc = sintOp(0, sintOp(2, acc, ten), sintRaw(0, d, 64, true))
        i = i + 1
    }
    // The Go twin accumulates in a uint64 and then reads the result as int64, so the
    // negation is the two's complement one at 64 bits.
    if (neg) { acc = sintOp(1, sintRaw(0, 0, 64, true), acc) }
    return csNorm(acc, w, u)
}

// csTypeObject: one of the integral type NAMES as an expression primary, so that
// `int.MaxValue`, `ulong.MaxValue` and `int.Parse(s)` resolve.
function csTypeObject(name0) {
    var o = {}
    if (name0 == "char" || name0 == "Char") {
        o["MinValue"] = csChar(0)
        o["MaxValue"] = csChar(65535)
        return o
    }
    var name = csTypeAlias(name0)
    var w = csTypeW(name)
    if (w == 0) { return o }
    var un = csTypeU(name)
    var lo = sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false)
    var hi = sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false)
    if (un) {
        // int64(-1) at width 64, i.e. every bit set; the narrower widths mask it.
        hi = csAllOnes()
    } else {
        // 2^(w-1) - 1 and its negation, built from the all-ones pattern so that no
        // exponent literal is needed.
        hi = sintOp(10, sintRaw(2147483647, 4294967295, 64, false), 64 - w)
        lo = sintOp(1, sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false), sintOp(0, hi, sintRaw(0, 1, 64, false)))
    }
    o["MinValue"] = csNorm(lo, w, un)
    o["MaxValue"] = csNorm(hi, w, un)
    o["Parse"] = function(s) { return csParseInt(cpStr(s), w, un) }
    return o
}

// ===================================================== the exports ==========
//
// Everything below is a TOP-LEVEL `function js_*`, which is what -rt-lib exports
// under its own C symbol. The order follows abnf/jsrtcsharp.go and the shared block
// of abnf/jsrt.go.

// ----- the language-NEUTRAL fifteen -----------------------------------------
// None of these is C#'s. They live in abnf/jsrt.go, several languages emit them,
// and they are here only because the C floor does not have them - see the extern
// split in docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3.

function js_arr_new_n(n, fill) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    var c = csNum(n)
    while (i < c) {
        out.push(fill)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// A char from its CODE. The code arrives as a RAW parameter - csharp-to-llvm-ir
// writes it with handle(k), so without the _raw suffix the callee would see the
// handle 0..3, i.e. undefined/null/false/true. The Lua pilot's finding.
function js_char(code_raw) { return csChar(code_raw) }

// The code of a char, and rt.toNumber for everything else. csharp-to-llvm-ir uses
// it as the operand guard in front of the FLOOR's js_csflo, which is the one place
// a floor extern would otherwise meet the {__char} box.
function js_char_code(v) {
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    return csNum(v)
}

// An empty Dictionary<K,V> / Python dict / Go map handle.
function js_dict_new() { return {__dict: true, keys: [], vals: []} }

function csIsDict(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return false }
    return v["__dict"] !== undefined
}

// The dict's key lookup, by VALUE - two boxes holding 5 are one key.
function csDictFind(keys, k) {
    var i = 0
    while (i < keys.length) {
        if (csValueEq(keys[i], k)) { return i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// rt.dictFind's equality: strict for everything the floor knows, by value for the
// two number tags and the char box.
function csValueEq(a, b) {
    if (csIsChar(a) || csIsChar(b)) { return js_jchareq(a, b) }
    if (floIs(a) || floIs(b)) { return floEq(a, b) }
    if (sintIs(a) || sintIs(b)) {
        if (!csIsIntegral(a)) { return false }
        if (!csIsIntegral(b)) { return false }
        return csCmp("==", a, b)
    }
    return a === b
}

// Sequence indexing and dict lookup. C#'s emitter routes every subscript through
// this (core.indexExt).
function js_pyget(c, k) {
    if (csIsDict(c)) {
        var i = csDictFind(c["keys"], k)
        if (i < 0) { fail("KeyError: " + cpStr(k)) }
        return c["vals"][i]
    }
    var idx = csNum(k)
    if (Array.isArray(c)) {
        if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
        if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("list index out of range") }
        return c[idx]
    }
    if (typeof c == "string") {
        if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
        if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("string index out of range") }
        return c.charAt(idx)
    }
    fail("indexing a " + typeof c)
    return undefined
}

function js_pyset(c, k, v) {
    if (csIsDict(c)) {
        var keys = c["keys"]
        var i = csDictFind(keys, k)
        if (i >= 0) { c["vals"][i] = v } else {
            keys.push(k)
            c["vals"].push(v)
        }
        return undefined
    }
    if (!Array.isArray(c)) { fail("item assignment on a " + typeof c) }
    var idx = csNum(k)
    if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
    if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("list assignment index out of range") }
    c[idx] = v
    return undefined
}

function js_pylen(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v.length }
    if (csIsDict(v)) { return v["keys"].length }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return v.length }
    fail("len() of a " + typeof v)
    return 0
}

// 'key in obj' / hasOwnProperty: own properties of an object, index and 'length' of
// an array or string. csharp-to-llvm-ir emits it for exactly one probe, "does this
// receiver carry a __class", so the object arm is the only one it reaches.
function js_has(o, key) {
    var k = cpToString(key)
    if (typeof o == "string") {
        if (k == "length") { return true }
        var si = csNum(key)
        if (si !== si) { return false }
        return si == Math.trunc(si) && si >= 0 && si < o.length
    }
    if (Array.isArray(o)) {
        if (k == "length") { return true }
        var ai = csNum(key)
        if (ai !== ai) { return false }
        return ai == Math.trunc(ai) && ai >= 0 && ai < o.length
    }
    if (o === undefined || o === null) { return false }
    if (typeof o != "object") { return false }
    if (o[k] !== undefined) { return true }
    return csFindAccessor(o, k)
}

// findClassAccessor: a getter/setter lives on the __class descriptor chain, not on
// the instance, so `key in instance` has to walk it.
function csFindAccessor(o, k) {
    var cls = o["__class"]
    var guard = 0
    while (guard < 64) {
        guard = guard + 1
        if (cls === undefined) { return false }
        if (cls === null) { return false }
        if (typeof cls != "object") { return false }
        if (cls["__get_" + k] !== undefined) { return true }
        if (cls["__set_" + k] !== undefined) { return true }
        cls = cls["__super"]
    }
    return false
}

// The dynamic type test behind `is` / a type pattern. abnf/jsrt.go's isTypeName is
// the spec, including the part that surprises: the Int/Long/Short/Byte arm tests for
// a PLAIN number, so a boxed long is NOT `is Int` there either.
function js_is_type(v, t) {
    var nm = t
    var lt = nm.indexOf("<")
    if (lt >= 0) { nm = nm.substring(0, lt) }
    var opt = false
    if (nm.length > 0) {
        if (nm.charAt(nm.length - 1) == "?") {
            nm = nm.substring(0, nm.length - 1)
            opt = true
        }
    }
    if (v === undefined) { return opt }
    if (v === null) { return opt }
    if (csIsChar(v)) {
        if (nm == "Char") { return true }
        if (nm == "Character") { return true }
        if (nm == "Any") { return true }
        return nm == "Comparable"
    }
    if (nm == "Any" || nm == "Object") { return true }
    if (nm == "Int" || nm == "Integer" || nm == "Long" || nm == "Short" || nm == "Byte" || nm == "Char" || nm == "Character") {
        if (!csIsPlainNum(v)) { return false }
        return Math.trunc(v) == v
    }
    if (nm == "Double" || nm == "Float" || nm == "Number") { return csIsPlainNum(v) }
    if (nm == "String" || nm == "CharSequence") { return typeof v == "string" }
    if (nm == "Boolean") { return typeof v == "boolean" }
    if (nm == "List" || nm == "MutableList" || nm == "Collection" || nm == "Array") { return Array.isArray(v) }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return false }
    var cls = v["__class"]
    var guard = 0
    while (guard < 64) {
        guard = guard + 1
        if (cls === undefined) { return false }
        if (cls === null) { return false }
        if (typeof cls != "object") { return false }
        if (cls["__name"] === nm) { return true }
        cls = cls["__super"]
    }
    return false
}

// The shared '+' (abnf/jsrt.go's js_jadd): string concat with Java's null
// rendering, 32 bit add for two int values, float add otherwise. The C# emitter
// reaches it only for index and length arithmetic it generates itself.
function js_jadd(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string") { return csJavaStr(l) + csJavaStr(r) }
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { return csFloArith("+", l, r) }
    var ln = csNum(l)
    var rn = csNum(r)
    if (csIsInt32Value(ln) && csIsInt32Value(rn)) { return csWrap32(ln + rn) }
    return ln + rn
}

// javaString: null/undefined print as "null" here, unlike C#'s own csString.
function csJavaStr(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return "null" }
    if (v === null) { return "null" }
    return cpToString(v)
}

function csIsInt32Value(n) {
    if (n !== n) { return false }
    if (Math.trunc(n) !== n) { return false }
    if (n > 2147483647) { return false }
    return n >= 0 - 2147483648
}

// C#'s == when a char may be involved. A char must compare equal to the
// one-character string the interpreter's String indexing answers, too. Without a
// char operand this is exactly js_seq.
function js_jchareq(l, r) {
    if (csIsChar(l)) {
        if (typeof r == "string") {
            if (r.length != 1) { return false }
            return r.charCodeAt(0) == l["__char"]
        }
        if (csIsChar(r)) { return l["__char"] === r["__char"] }
        if (csIsPlainNum(r)) { return l["__char"] === r }
        if (sintIs(r)) { return csCmp("==", l, r) }
        if (floIs(r)) { return floEq(flo(l["__char"], 2), r) }
        return false
    }
    if (csIsChar(r)) { return js_jchareq(r, l) }
    return csStrictEq(l, r)
}

// The floor's === with the two number boxes taught to it. floEq is jvmNumEq, whose
// jsGInt case is deliberately absent (a boxed double against a SIZED INTEGER is
// false), and that asymmetry is reproduced rather than smoothed over.
function csStrictEq(l, r) {
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { return floEq(l, r) }
    if (sintIs(l) || sintIs(r)) {
        if (!csIsIntegral(l)) { return false }
        if (!csIsIntegral(r)) { return false }
        return csCmp("==", l, r)
    }
    return l === r
}

// (target, method name, args array). The Go twin (rt.memberCall in abnf/jsrt.go) is
// a LANGUAGE-NEUTRAL dispatcher; only the arms csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf can reach are
// here, and the ones it cannot are named at the fallthrough rather than guessed at.
function js_mcall(recv, name, args) {
    if (typeof recv == "string") {
        if (name == "length") { return recv.length }
        if (name == "charAt") { return recv.charAt(csNum(args[0])) }
        if (name == "equals") { return csStrictEq(recv, args[0]) }
        if (name == "substring") {
            var b = args.length > 0 ? csNum(args[0]) : 0
            var e = args.length > 1 ? csNum(args[1]) : recv.length
            return csSubstring(recv, b, e)
        }
        if (name == "indexOf") { return recv.indexOf(cpToString(args[0])) }
        if (name == "contains") { return recv.indexOf(cpToString(args[0])) >= 0 }
        if (name == "toUpperCase" || name == "ToUpper") { return recv.toUpperCase() }
        if (name == "toLowerCase" || name == "ToLower") { return recv.toLowerCase() }
        if (name == "trim" || name == "Trim") { return recv.trim() }
        if (name == "isEmpty") { return recv.length == 0 }
        fail("unknown String method: " + name)
        return undefined
    }
    if (recv === undefined || recv === null) {
        fail("method call '" + name + "' on a " + (recv === null ? "null" : "undefined"))
        return undefined
    }
    if (typeof recv != "object") {
        fail("method call '" + name + "' on a " + typeof recv)
        return undefined
    }
    if (Array.isArray(recv)) { return csListMethod(recv, name, args) }
    if (csIsDict(recv)) { return csDictMethod(recv, name, args) }
    if (csIsChar(recv)) {
        fail("method call '" + name + "' on a char")
        return undefined
    }
    var cls = recv["__class"]
    if (cls !== undefined) {
        var c = cls
        var guard = 0
        while (guard < 64) {
            guard = guard + 1
            if (c === undefined) { guard = 99 } else if (c === null) { guard = 99 } else if (typeof c != "object") { guard = 99 } else {
                var mm = c[name]
                if (csIsFn(mm)) { return mm.apply(undefined, csPrepend(recv, args)) }
                c = c["__super"]
            }
        }
        fail("unknown method '" + name + "' on an instance")
        return undefined
    }
    var f = recv[name]
    if (csIsFn(f)) { return f.apply(undefined, args) }
    fail("unknown method '" + name + "'")
    return undefined
}

function csListMethod(recv, name, args) {
    if (name == "length" || name == "size" || name == "count") { return recv.length }
    if (name == "add" || name == "push" || name == "append") {
        recv.push(args[0])
        return true
    }
    if (name == "get") {
        var gi = csNum(args[0])
        if (gi < 0 || gi >= recv.length) {
            fail("list index " + gi + " out of range")
            return undefined
        }
        return recv[gi]
    }
    if (name == "contains" || name == "includes") {
        var k = 0
        while (k < recv.length) {
            if (csValueEq(recv[k], args[0])) { return true }
            k = k + 1
        }
        return false
    }
    if (name == "indexOf") {
        var j = 0
        while (j < recv.length) {
            if (csValueEq(recv[j], args[0])) { return j }
            j = j + 1
        }
        return 0 - 1
    }
    fail("unknown list method '" + name + "'")
    return undefined
}

// A Dictionary's keys / values / contains, which csharp-to-llvm-ir emits for
// '.Keys', '.Values' and '.ContainsKey'.
function csDictMethod(recv, name, args) {
    if (name == "keys") { return csCopyArr(recv["keys"]) }
    if (name == "values") { return csCopyArr(recv["vals"]) }
    if (name == "length" || name == "size" || name == "count") { return recv["keys"].length }
    if (name == "contains" || name == "containsKey") { return csDictFind(recv["keys"], args[0]) >= 0 }
    if (name == "add") {
        js_pyset(recv, args[0], args[1])
        return undefined
    }
    fail("unknown dictionary method '" + name + "'")
    return undefined
}

function csCopyArr(a) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        out.push(a[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// csIsFn: "may this member be called". `typeof v == "function"` is the whole
// test, in both halves: a generator function (the C floor's tag 16, a *hostFunc
// in the Go twin) answers "function" as of the floor fix, so a static iterator
// method is callable through js_mcall natively exactly as it is under llvm.Run.
// This used to probe `typeof v["apply"] == "function"` instead, because the C
// floor's type_of fell through to "object" for tag 16 while is_callable was
// already true - that WORKAROUND is gone with the floor line that caused it.
function csIsFn(v) {
    return typeof v == "function"
}

function csPrepend(v, args) {
    var out = [v]
    var i = 0
    while (i < args.length) {
        out.push(args[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// String.Substring with the Go twin's substringRange clamping.
function csSubstring(s, b, e) {
    var begin = b
    var end = e
    if (begin < 0) { begin = 0 }
    if (end > s.length) { end = s.length }
    if (end < begin) { end = begin }
    return s.substring(begin, end)
}

// (super class, this, method name, args array). The walk starts AT the given class:
// the emitter already resolved the superclass of the DEFINING class, so a subclass
// override never shadows what a hidden-name call means.
function js_supercall(sup, self, name, args) {
    var cls = sup
    var guard = 0
    while (guard < 64) {
        guard = guard + 1
        if (cls === undefined) { guard = 99 } else if (cls === null) { guard = 99 } else if (typeof cls != "object") { guard = 99 } else {
            var m = cls[name]
            if (csIsFn(m)) { return m.apply(undefined, csPrepend(self, args)) }
            cls = cls["__super"]
        }
    }
    fail("unknown super method '" + name + "'")
    return undefined
}

// s[lo..hi] on a string or an array: the elements are COPIED. An absent bound
// arrives as undefined and reads as 0 / the length.
function js_goslice(v, lo0, hi0) {
    if (typeof v == "string") {
        var slo = lo0 === undefined ? 0 : csNum(lo0)
        var shi = hi0 === undefined ? v.length : csNum(hi0)
        if (slo < 0 || shi > v.length || slo > shi) { fail("slice bounds [" + slo + ":" + shi + "] out of range") }
        return v.substring(slo, shi)
    }
    if (!Array.isArray(v)) {
        fail("slicing a " + typeof v)
        return undefined
    }
    var lo = lo0 === undefined ? 0 : csNum(lo0)
    var hi = hi0 === undefined ? v.length : csNum(hi0)
    if (lo < 0 || hi > v.length || lo > hi) { fail("slice bounds [" + lo + ":" + hi + "] out of range") }
    var out = []
    var i = lo
    while (i < hi) {
        out.push(v[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// f(xs...): flatten the LAST element of the argument array IN PLACE, so the callee
// sees the array's elements as separate arguments. The Go twin re-slices; this
// dialect has no splice, so the tail is popped and pushed back one element at a
// time - the same array object, which is what the caller holds.
function js_gospread(args) {
    if (!Array.isArray(args)) { return undefined }
    if (args.length == 0) { return undefined }
    var last = args.pop()
    if (Array.isArray(last)) {
        var i = 0
        while (i < last.length) {
            args.push(last[i])
            i = i + 1
        }
        return undefined
    }
    if (last === null) { return undefined }
    if (last === undefined) { return undefined }
    args.push(last)
    return undefined
}

// ----- C#'s own eighteen ----------------------------------------------------

// One binary integral operator: (op, left, right). A double operand keeps the FLOAT
// path of abnf/jsrtjvm.go, which is where the two number tags meet; two bools take
// C#'s non-short-circuit & | ^.
function js_csarith(op, l, r) {
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { return csFloArith(op, l, r) }
    if (typeof l == "boolean") {
        if (typeof r == "boolean") {
            if (op == "&") { return l && r }
            if (op == "|") { return l || r }
            if (op == "^") { return l !== r }
        }
    }
    if (!csIsIntegral(l) || !csIsIntegral(r)) { return csFloArith(op, l, r) }
    return csArith(op, l, r)
}

function js_csshift(op, l, r) { return csShift(op, l, r) }

function js_cscmp(op, l, r) {
    if (csIsIntegral(l) && csIsIntegral(r) && !floIs(l) && !floIs(r)) { return csCmp(op, l, r) }
    var c = csCompare(l, r)
    if (op == "<") { return c < 0 }
    if (op == ">") { return c > 0 }
    if (op == "<=") { return c <= 0 }
    return c >= 0
}

// A cast or a declared integral type: (value, bits, unsigned).
function js_csconv(v, bits, uns) { return csConvTo(v, csNum(bits), uns === true) }

// A declared type applied to an initializer: only an INTEGRAL value converts, so
// `object o = "s"` and a lambda pass through untouched.
function js_csadopt(v, bits, uns) {
    if (!csIsIntegral(v)) { return v }
    return csConvTo(v, csNum(bits), uns === true)
}

// An integer literal, passed as DIGITS because emitNum would already have rounded
// 9223372036854775807 to 9223372036854776000 on the way into the module. Its TYPE
// follows its suffix and its value (ECMA-334 6.4.5.3): with no suffix it is the
// first of int, uint, long, ulong that holds it; U makes it uint or ulong; L long or
// ulong; UL ulong.
function js_cslit(text, radix, hasU, hasL) {
    var rx = csNum(radix)
    var acc = sintRaw(0, 0, 64, true)
    var rb = sintRaw(0, rx, 64, true)
    var i = 0
    while (i < text.length) {
        var c = text.charCodeAt(i)
        var d = 0 - 1
        if (c >= 48 && c <= 57) { d = c - 48 } else if (c >= 97 && c <= 102) { d = c - 97 + 10 } else if (c >= 65 && c <= 70) { d = c - 65 + 10 }
        if (d >= 0 && d < rx) { acc = sintOp(0, sintOp(2, acc, rb), sintRaw(0, d, 64, true)) }
        i = i + 1
    }
    var hi = sintHi(acc)
    var lo = sintLo(acc)
    var fitsUInt = hi == 0
    var fitsInt = hi == 0 && lo <= 2147483647
    var fitsLong = hi <= 2147483647
    var u = hasU === true
    var el = hasL === true
    if (u && el) { return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, true) }
    if (u) {
        if (fitsUInt) { return sintRaw(hi, lo, 32, true) }
        return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, true)
    }
    if (el) {
        if (fitsLong) { return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, false) }
        return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, true)
    }
    if (fitsInt) { return lo }
    if (fitsUInt) { return sintRaw(hi, lo, 32, true) }
    if (fitsLong) { return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, false) }
    return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, true)
}

// -x, at the UNARY promoted type (ECMA-334 12.4.7.2): a long keeps its type, a uint
// becomes a LONG (because -uint.MaxValue does not fit in a uint) and everything
// narrower becomes an int.
function js_csneg(v) {
    // NOT `flo(0 - floNum(v), 2)`. Unary minus on a double is emitted as `0.0 - x`
    // by languages/c-to-llvm-ir.abnf, and 0.0 - 0.0 is +0.0 - so the sign of a
    // NEGATIVE ZERO would be lost. Multiplying by -1.0 computes the sign instead of
    // writing it, and is exact for every operand including the infinities and NaN.
    if (floIs(v)) { return floOp(2, v, flo(0 - 1, 2)) }
    if (csSzW(v) == 64) { return csNorm(csNegate(v, 64, csSzU(v)), 64, csSzU(v)) }
    if (csSzW(v) == 32 && csSzU(v)) { return csNorm(csNegate(v, 64, false), 64, false) }
    return csWrap32(0 - csNorm(v, 32, false))
}

// Two's complement negation at a given width, which maps MinValue to itself exactly
// as C#'s unchecked unary minus does.
function csNegate(v, w, u) {
    return sintOp(1, csBox(0, w, u), csBox(v, w, u))
}

// ~x, at the same unary promoted type.
function js_csnot(v) {
    if (csSzW(v) == 64) { return csNorm(csComplement(v, 64, csSzU(v)), 64, csSzU(v)) }
    if (csSzW(v) == 32 && csSzU(v)) { return csNorm(csComplement(v, 32, true), 32, true) }
    return csWrap32(0 - csNorm(v, 32, false) - 1)
}

function csComplement(v, w, u) {
    return sintOp(7, csBox(v, w, u), csBox(csAllOnes(), w, u))
}

// ++ / -- step at the operand's OWN type and wrap there (ECMA-334 12.8.15: `x++` is
// `x = (T)(x + 1)`), so `byte b = 255; b++` is 0.
function js_csstep(v, d) {
    var n = csNum(d)
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return csChar((v["__char"] + n) & 65535) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return flo(floNum(v) + n, floStyle(v)) }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return csConvTo(csArith("+", v, n), sintWidth(v), sintUns(v)) }
    return csWrap32(csNorm(v, 32, false) + n)
}

// The result of a COMPOUND assignment: the operation, then the implicit cast back to
// the LEFT operand's type (ECMA-334 12.21.4: `x op= y` is `x = (T)(x op y)`), so
// `byte b = 250; b += 10` is (byte)260 == 4.
function js_csnarrowlike(l, v) {
    if (floIs(l)) {
        if (floIs(v)) { return v }
        return flo(csNum(v), floStyle(l))
    }
    if (csIsChar(l)) { return csChar(csNorm(v, 16, true)) }
    if (sintIs(l)) { return csConvTo(v, sintWidth(l), sintUns(l)) }
    if (csIsPlainNum(l)) {
        if (csIsIntegral(v) || floIs(v)) { return csConvTo(v, 32, false) }
    }
    return v
}

// A (char) cast: narrow to 16 UNSIGNED bits and re-box.
function js_cschar(v) {
    if (floIs(v)) { return csChar(csNorm(csConvTo(v, 16, true), 16, true)) }
    return csChar(csNorm(v, 16, true))
}

// C#'s '+'. Identical to the shared js_csadd - string concat with C#'s null rule,
// float add when a double is involved - except that two INTEGRAL operands add at
// their promoted type and wrap to it, instead of through a double that rounds above
// 2^53.
function js_csadd2(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string") { return cpAddStr(l) + cpAddStr(r) }
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { return flo(csNum(l) + csNum(r), csFloStyle(l, r)) }
    if (csIsIntegral(l) && csIsIntegral(r)) { return csArith("+", l, r) }
    return csNum(l) + csNum(r)
}

// jvmStyleOf: the style of whichever operand is a box, C# (2) when neither is.
function csFloStyle(l, r) {
    if (floIs(l)) { return floStyle(l) }
    if (floIs(r)) { return floStyle(r) }
    return 2
}

// The shared js_csadd (abnf/jsrt.go): the emitter uses it for `"" + x`, which is
// what .ToString() lowers to.
function js_csadd(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string") { return cpAddStr(l) + cpAddStr(r) }
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { return flo(csNum(l) + csNum(r), csFloStyle(l, r)) }
    var ln = csNum(l)
    var rn = csNum(r)
    if (csIsInt32Value(ln) && csIsInt32Value(rn)) { return csWrap32(ln + rn) }
    return ln + rn
}

// == / != with a sized integer on either side: two integers compare BY VALUE at
// their promoted type, so a uint and a long holding the same number are equal and
// two boxes holding 5 are not two distinct objects. The third argument is the
// js_jchareq / $valeq answer the caller already computed.
function js_cseq(l, r, pre) {
    if (csIsIntegral(l) && csIsIntegral(r) && !floIs(l) && !floIs(r)) {
        if (sintIs(l)) { return csCmp("==", l, r) }
        if (sintIs(r)) { return csCmp("==", l, r) }
    }
    return pre
}

// One of the integral type NAMES as an expression primary.
function js_cstype(name) { return csTypeObject(name) }

// C#'s .Length / .Count. A string's Length counts UTF-16 CODE UNITS (System.String
// is a UTF-16 sequence, so "😀".Length is 2) - which is what a MetaJS
// string's .length already counts. Everything else is js_pylen.
function js_cslen(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v.length }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return v.length }
    if (csIsDict(v)) { return v["keys"].length }
    fail("Length/Count of a " + typeof v)
    return 0
}

// The whole Console object, built here rather than in the grammar so that WriteLine
// / Write render through cpStr instead of through the shared println host function.
function js_csconsole() {
    var o = {}
    o["WriteLine"] = function(v) { println(cpStr(v)) }
    o["Write"] = function(v) { print(cpStr(v)) }
    return o
}

// The rendering step of a string CONVERSION, used by '+', by an interpolation hole
// and by .ToString(). It converts only the values whose C# string form differs from
// the shared runtime's - a bool, an array and a class instance - and passes
// everything else through UNCHANGED, so that `1 + 2` stays an integer addition and a
// boxed double stays a double. The caller wraps the result in js_csadd, which
// supplies C#'s null rule and the numeric cases.
function js_csstr(v) {
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return cpBoolStr(v) }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return cpArrayName(v) }
    if (v === undefined) { return v }
    if (v === null) { return v }
    if (typeof v == "object") {
        if (csIsChar(v)) { return v }
        var s = cpObjStr(v, 0)
        if (s !== undefined) { return s }
    }
    return v
}
